From 039735cbfc71336c82719fd483a606a979d09502 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 21:59:42 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 14/15] gas: microblaze: emit line advances as relocations

gas writes each address advance in a line table it generates from .loc
as a special opcode or DW_LNS_advance_pc, with the delta computed at
assembly time.  Linker relaxation then deletes IMMs, and every row
after a deleted IMM is too late.  In the new test, a function loses two
IMMs and its line 2 row stays at 0x90000008, after the deleted IMM
rather than at the brlid at 0x90000004, and the sequence ends inside
the next function.

Define DWARF2_USE_FIXED_ADVANCE_PC, as tc-riscv.h does.  dwarf2dbg.c
then emits each advance as DW_LNS_fixed_advance_pc with a 2-byte
difference of code labels, which md_apply_fix already turns into an
ADD16/SUB16 pair.  For a delta over 50000 it resets the address with
DW_LNE_set_address instead, so relaxation cannot overflow the field.

Line tables get larger: three bytes per advance instead of one for most
rows.  Because gas no longer resolves the deltas, it can no longer
prove that two rows have different addresses, so it emits view
numbers where it did not before.  That changes the output of eleven
generic DWARF tests, which already xfail for the other targets that do
not resolve these differences at assembly time (and for riscv); add
microblaze to those lists, and run lns-common-1-alt and lns-big-delta
for it as lns.exp does for riscv.

GCC's own line tables are not affected: MicroBlaze GCC sets
DWARF2_ASM_LINE_DEBUG_INFO to 0 and does not use .loc.

